//// A lambda whose WHOLE body is one expression answers that expression's
//// value. The lambda's last statement is its return position, and every form
//// the language gives a value to lands there: an `if`, a `case`, a
//// `try … catch` (a loop is a statement — decision 105).
////
//// Measured by emilia's theme front on commonJS, where `{ x -> if (c) { a }
//// else { b } }` answered `undefined` for every element: the emitter wrapped
//// the `if` in the value IIFE it uses everywhere else and then emitted that
//// IIFE as a STATEMENT — `(x) => { (() => { … })(); }` — so the arrow returned
//// nothing. erlang and wasm were unaffected, which is why the same source was
//// green on one target and silently wrong on another. The workaround a library
//// had to write was binding the `if` to a `val` and yielding that.
////
//// The `case` row is the control: it never had the defect, and it is here so a
//// regression that fixes one form and breaks the other is visible.
////
//// Each result is read as `join(",")` rather than printed as an array on
//// purpose: `@print` of an array is decision 8 §7's separator row, which two
//// backends still get wrong, and a §7 row must not hide this one. A cell that
//// only checked the program RAN would have passed before the fix — the defect
//// is a wrong answer, not a crash — so every line asserts the VALUE.

#[@result]
fn tenth(x: i32) -> @Result<i32, string> {
    if (x > 1) { return x * 10; } else { throw "too small"; }
}

pub fn main() {
    val xs = [1, 2, 3];

    @print(xs.map({ x -> if (x > 1) { x * 10 } else { x } }).join(","));

    @print(xs.map({ x -> case x { 1 { 100 } _ { 200 } } }).join(","));

    @print(xs.map({ x -> try tenth(x) catch 0 }).join(","));
}
